WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.040
YUFENG GUO: In
previous videos, you've

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.800
seen me running Python
code live on screen

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.540
and showing the results.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.550
On this episode of
"AI Adventures,"

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.220
I'll show you how to use
Jupyter notebooks to achieve

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.230
the same results.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.079
The way I've been running
Python code live on screen

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.180
is by using a Python
package called Jupyter.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.930
Jupyter is built on
the IPython project

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.760
and allows for
interactive Python

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.050
to be run right in your browser.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.990
But it is so much
more than that.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.410
From bash commands, to
special magics, and plugins,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.550
Jupyter enhances the Python
coding experience greatly.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.350
If you're already
using Jupyter, I

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.450
hope I can improve your workflow
and show you some new tricks.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.740
And if you're not using Jupyter
yet, what are you waiting for?

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.330
The easiest way
to install Jupyter

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.320
is by running pip
install jupyter.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.620
Though, if you use
a packaged Python

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.500
distribution, such as Anaconda,
you may already have it

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.520
installed.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.240
So be sure to activate your
Python environment first.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.740
And let's dive in.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.570
When running Jupyter
locally, you'll

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.390
connect to a locally-running
web server through your browser,

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.920
typically on port 8888.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.460
Start your notebook by
running Jupyter notebook

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.110
in your working directory.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.345
And usually, Jupyter will
just automatically open

00:01:12.345 --> 00:01:14.100
in your browser
once it starts up.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.315
But if not, point your browser
over to localhost 8888.

00:01:18.315 --> 00:01:20.940
And if you don't already have a
notebook that you want to open,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.130
you can create one
by clicking on New

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.261
and selecting Python
2 or Python 3,

00:01:25.261 --> 00:01:26.760
depending on which
version of Python

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.890
you have running in
your environment.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.950
Jupyter is quite flexible
and can handle many languages

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:32.880
and file types.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.790
Though, we'll just stick
with Python for now.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.530
Once you have a new
notebook running,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.050
you can write some Python
code in the empty cell.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.840
And just hit
Control-Enter to run it.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.240
We can also run all our
typical Python code here,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.430
just like you might
write in a Python script.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.770
The difference is that
we can run it and see

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.930
the results right away.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.082
Notice what happens when we
run a cell with Control-Enter.

00:01:55.082 --> 00:01:56.790
The brackets on the
left side of the cell

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.570
shows an asterisk when a cell
is running or queued to run,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.730
and then shows a number
once it's finished,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.460
representing the order in which
cells were run during a given

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.560
session starting at 1.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.660
The results of the final
line of a code cell

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.340
will be printed as an
output of that cell

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.760
but only if the value is
not stored to a variable.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.680
So for example, if I import
TensorFlow and then concatenate

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.820
its version with a string, the
output is shown below the cell,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.160
even though I didn't
use the Print command.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.470
And of course, you can
use the Print command.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.550
And it would work
fine just as well.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.570
Now, this is all very useful
for tinkering and seeing

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.820
how something behaves.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.040
Another fantastic feature
of Jupyter notebooks

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.700
is the ability to show the
docstring of a function

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:44.274
that you are calling by
just pressing Shift-Tab.

00:02:44.274 --> 00:02:45.690
This allows you
to call a function

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.440
with the correct
arguments without needing

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.350
to look up the
full documentation

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:50.970
every single time.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.700
This feature also works with
your local custom functions.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.320
So if you write good
docstrings, you'll be rewarded.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.880
When you have a
lot of output, you

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.830
can reduce the amount
of space it takes up

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.470
by clicking on the left-hand
panel of the output, which

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.840
turns it into a
scrolling window.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.860
If you double click, the output
will be collapsed entirely.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.180
One cell is useful.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.610
But really, we want
to have many cells.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.610
To add a cell, click the
plus icon in the toolbar.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.500
There are also some
cell execution commands

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.950
which can lead to new
cells being created for us.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.370
If you press Shift-Enter,
it will run the current cell

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.750
and then just highlight
that next cell.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.540
But if there is
no new next cell,

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.510
then a new cell is created.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.850
On the other hand, if you
want to insert a new cell

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.130
immediately after
a given cell, you

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.350
can use Alt-Enter
to execute the cell

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.380
and then insert a new
cell directly after.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.170
Perhaps the biggest feature
that I've left out so far

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.760
is the markdown support.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.530
My first impression
of Jupyter notebooks

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.080
was its ability to
provide a great way

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.280
to both write code and describe
the code I was writing.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.590
The rich semantics
of markdown allow

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.580
for researchers and educators
alike to easily and cleanly

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.380
communicate thoughts and ideas.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.380
But perhaps, most
importantly, it

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.102
allows past you
to tell future you

00:04:09.102 --> 00:04:10.560
what a given code
cell was supposed

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.890
to do in a way that can be
much more expressive than using

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.750
comment blocks.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.510
Sometimes I just want to do
a quick check on how long

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.200
one training or evaluation
cycle is taking to execute.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.420
For an easy way
to time your code,

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.450
start a cell with
percent percent time.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.250
And once the cell
finishes executing,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.790
it will print out how long
it took to run that cell.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.770
It's not precision
atomic timing,

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.290
but it is a great way to get
some solid first impressions

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.670
with very minimal effort.

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:43.300
If you want to run a command
line command in your notebook,

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.880
the easiest way is to
put an exclamation point

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.380
in front of the command.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.170
Now, this is only really
useful for one-off commands.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.850
If you want to run
a bunch of commands,

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:55.120
start a cell with
percent percent bash

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.930
to cause the entire cell to be
interpreted as a bash script.

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.080
A great use of this feature
is to kick off TensorBoard.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.760
Typically, running
TensorBoard might

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.590
involve starting a
new terminal window

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:07.300
and running it on the
command line, which

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.050
is what we would
typically do if we wanted

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:10.820
it to run for a long time.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:13.540
But if we just want to
spin it up, take a peek,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:17.020
and close it back down, having
it in a Jupyter notebook cell

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.430
isn't such a bad idea.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:20.860
Plus, you'll never
forget to run it,

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:24.280
since it's embedded in your
workflow of notebook cells.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.530
Notice that it will
occupy your notebook.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:27.790
See that asterisk?

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:29.710
So you won't be able
to run anything else

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.660
while TensorBoard is running.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.270
To stop it, click
Interrupt Kernel.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.916
The asterisk will go away.

00:05:35.916 --> 00:05:37.290
And you'll get
back control flow.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.450
So there you have it, some of
my favorite Jupyter features

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:46.930
and capabilities.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.260
This is, of course, not a
comprehensive discussion

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.779
of all of Jupyter's features.

00:05:50.779 --> 00:05:52.320
I've just covered
some of my favorite

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.340
and most frequently used here.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.300
There are many, many more
waiting for you to explore.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.510
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:02.100
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.200
to catch future episodes
right when they come out.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.740
But for now, try out
Jupyter notebooks and all

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.100
that they have to offer.

